{-
   What the linear rules refuse, and the exact words they refuse it in.

   # resolver: ownership

   The whole of `@explicitDrop` is a diagnostic, so this file is the feature rather than a corner of
   it: nothing about a linear type changes what a correct program compiles to, and every guarantee it
   offers is a program that stops compiling. Each function below is wrong in one way, and the
   interesting property is that each gets *its own* message - a pass that reported "this value must
   be consumed" for all of them would pass a golden and help nobody.

   `Reject.Moves.yana` is the neighbouring file and the split between them is not thematic: the
   messages there come from a value being used *after* it was handed over, and the ones here from a
   value never being handed over at all.

   What a *disposal* is refused for is in `Reject.Disposal.yana`, and that split is mechanical rather
   than thematic: those come from the resolver, resolution stops before the analysis runs, and one
   file cannot show both.
-}

let &trace = 0 :: I32

@explicitDrop
data Guard {id: I32}

instance Drop(Guard):
    fn drop(->value: Guard):
        trace = trace + value.id

fn guard(id: I32) -> Guard = Guard {id: id}

fn idOf(g: Guard) -> I32 = g.id

fn eat(->g: Guard) -> I32:
    let n = g.id
    ->g
    return n

-- The plain case: made, read, and left to die. This is the point the compiler would have inserted a
-- teardown, and the attribute is the statement that it may not.
fn forgotten() -> I32:
    let g = guard(1)
    return idOf(g)

-- A `->` parameter that consumes nothing. The signature says ownership arrives here, and a body that
-- does not pass it on is the same mistake as the one above with the value arriving from a caller.
fn consumedNowhere(->g: Guard) -> I32 = g.id

-- Disposed of on one arm and not on the other. A drop flag is what would make this work at run time,
-- and `doc/spec/ownership.md` refuses one deliberately: a value whose disposal the source has to
-- write may not be disposed of at a point only a run-time bit knows about.
--
-- The caret is on the `if` rather than on the `->`, and that is right: the arm that disposes of it is
-- correct, and the value dies on the one that does not. Drops are placed at the *earliest* point a
-- value is owned and dead, which for a branch is inside the arm - so the state at the join is never
-- the conditional one, and the message is the ordinary one about the path that forgot.
fn conditional(flag: Bool) -> I32:
    let g = guard(1)
    if flag:
        ->g

    return 0

data Pair {left: Guard, right: Guard}

-- One member disposed of and the other not. The record propagated the obligation, so the message
-- names the *path* rather than the binding - which is the distinction partial ownership exists to
-- make, and it is what tells an author which of the two they forgot.
fn halfDisposed() -> I32:
    let p = Pair {left: guard(1), right: guard(2)}
    ->p.left
    return 0

data Pool {held: Guard}

-- The rule an authored `Drop` takes on by existing. `Pool` is an ordinary value *because* it writes
-- one, so the body is what answers for the member - and a body that does not is where the obligation
-- would otherwise vanish with nothing said. Reported inside the teardown rather than at the holder,
-- because that is the only place it can be fixed.
instance Drop(Pool):
    fn drop(->value: Pool):
        trace = trace + 1

fn main() -> I32 = 0
